/*!
@defgroup hcu_v1
@ingroup mod_security Hardware Cryptography Unit V1 (HCU_V1)
@brief Hardware-assisted block cipher, MAC, and hash engine for HCU V1 devices.

@details
The HCU V1 module exposes a host-side driver for the Hardware Cryptography Unit.
The driver coordinates key loading, message framing, FIFO movement, DMA or
interrupt based carry modes, and operation-complete handling for the HCU V1
security engine. Depending on the enabled device features, the module supports
AES block-cipher flows, SM4 ECB processing, CMAC, CCM, SHA-256, SHA-384, and
optional HMAC services.

@section hcu_v1_features Key Features
- AES encryption and decryption support for ECB, CBC, CTR, CCM, and CMAC flows.
- Optional SM4 ECB processing through the same runtime state machine.
- SHA-256 and SHA-384 streaming support, including optional authorize flows.
- Optional HMAC key loading and HMAC generation or authorization wrappers.
- Three data-carry modes selected through `hcu_user_config_t::carryType`:
  polling, interrupt driven FIFO service, and DMA transfer.
- Configurable input data swapping to match the byte or bit ordering expected
  by the hardware data path.
- Callback-based completion path for asynchronous interrupt and DMA operation.

@section hcu_v1_modes Supported Modes

The table below summarizes the operation families exposed by the public driver.
Availability depends on the corresponding `FEATURE_HCU_*` macros.

| Operation Family | Public APIs | Feature Guard | Notes |
|------------------|-------------|---------------|-------|
| AES ECB | `HCU_DRV_EncryptECB()`, `HCU_DRV_DecryptECB()` | Always present in this driver variant | Buffers must be 32-bit aligned and lengths must be multiples of 16 bytes. |
| AES CBC | `HCU_DRV_EncryptCBC()`, `HCU_DRV_DecryptCBC()` | Always present in this driver variant | Optional IV pointer updates the context when non-NULL. |
| AES CTR | `HCU_DRV_EncryptCTR()`, `HCU_DRV_DecryptCTR()` | `FEATURE_HCU_AES_CTR_ENGINE` | Optional counter-value pointer updates the context when non-NULL. |
| AES CMAC | `HCU_DRV_GenerateMAC()`, `HCU_DRV_AuthorizeMAC()` | Always present in this driver variant | `msgType` controls segmented-message handling. |
| AES CCM | `HCU_DRV_CCMConfig()`, `HCU_DRV_EncryptCCM()`, `HCU_DRV_DecryptCCM()` | `FEATURE_HCU_AES_CCM_ENGINE` | `HCU_DRV_CCMConfig()` must be called before CCM payload processing. |
| SM4 ECB | `HCU_DRV_EncryptSM4ECB()`, `HCU_DRV_DecryptSM4ECB()` | `FEATURE_HCU_SM4_ENGINE` | Shares the same FIFO service model as AES ECB. |
| SHA | `HCU_DRV_GenerateSHA()`, `HCU_DRV_AuthorizeSHA()` | `FEATURE_HCU_SHA_ENGINE` | SHA-256 and SHA-384 are selected through `hcu_sha_type_t`. |
| HMAC | `HCU_DRV_LoadHMACKey()`, `HCU_DRV_GenerateHMAC()`, `HCU_DRV_AuthorizeHMAC()` | `FEATURE_HCU_HMAC_ENGINE` | Generation and authorization reuse the SHA execution path. |

@section hcu_v1_arch Module Architecture

The HCU V1 software stack is organized into a three-part structure:

@verbatim
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                 Application Code                 │
│         (calls HCU_DRV_* public APIs)            │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│          HCU Driver Layer (hcu_driver.h/.c)      │
│  ● Runtime state management and key loading      │
│  ● Polling, interrupt, and DMA carry selection   │
│  ● Cipher, MAC, hash, and CCM orchestration      │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│       HCU HW Access Layer (hcu_hw_access.h)      │
│  ● Inline register helpers for status, FIFO,     │
│    interrupt, key, IV/CV, DMA, and HMAC control  │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│        HCU IRQ Wrapper (hcu_irq.c)               │
│  ● Startup-visible ISR entry forwarding          │
│    into HCU_DRV_IRQHandler()                     │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│          HCU V1 Peripheral Hardware              │
│  ● Control, FIFO, MAC, SHA, and DMA registers    │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
@endverbatim

@subsection hcu_v1_arch_driver Driver Layer (hcu_driver.h / hcu_driver.c)
The driver layer owns the single runtime state pointer, validates user buffers,
programs the active engine and algorithm, and services the command through the
selected carry mode. Polling mode blocks until the command finishes, while
interrupt and DMA modes return after the engine is started.

@subsection hcu_v1_arch_hw HW Access Layer (hcu_hw_access.h)
The hardware access layer provides inline helpers grouped around:
- **Status and Interrupt Control** - status-flag reads, W1C helpers, and IRQ enables.
- **Engine and Context Configuration** - algorithm, key-size, IV/CV, MAC, and length programming.
- **FIFO Runtime Access** - FIFO reset, FIFO readiness checks, and data movement helpers.
- **DMA, Verification, and Reset Control** - DMA enables, SHA verification, APB reset, and optional HMAC helpers.

@section hcu_v1_config Configuration Parameters

@subsection hcu_v1_config_user hcu_user_config_t Fields
| Field | Type | Description | Default / Expected Value |
|-------|------|-------------|--------------------------|
| swap | `hcu_swapping_t` | Input data swap mode written into the HCU control register. | Caller assigned |
| carryType | `hcu_carry_type_t` | Selects polling, interrupt, or DMA based FIFO service. | Caller assigned |
| ingressDMAChannel | `uint8_t` | DMA channel used for input FIFO service when `carryType` is DMA. | Caller assigned |
| egressDMAChannel | `uint8_t` | DMA channel used for output FIFO service when `carryType` is DMA. | Caller assigned |

@subsection hcu_v1_config_ccm hcu_ccm_config_t Fields
| Field | Type | Description | Notes |
|-------|------|-------------|-------|
| nonce | `uint8_t *` | Nonce buffer used to build the CCM B0 block and counter value. | Must remain valid while the command runs. |
| nonceSize | `uint8_t` | Nonce length in bytes. | Combined with the encoded length field to satisfy CCM formatting rules. |
| addData | `uint8_t *` | Additional authenticated data buffer. | May be NULL when `addDataSize` is zero. |
| addDataSize | `uint8_t` | Additional authenticated data length in bytes. | Encoded into the AAD prefix when non-zero. |
| tag | `uint8_t *` | Authentication tag buffer. | Used as input for decrypt/authenticate and output for encrypt/generate. |
| tagSize | `uint8_t` | Authentication tag length in bytes. | Must be even and must not exceed 16. |
| msgLen | `uint64_t` | Total CCM payload length in bytes. | Written into the message-total register during setup. |

@subsection hcu_v1_config_cmac hcu_cmac_config_t Fields
| Field | Type | Description |
|-------|------|-------------|
| macPtr | `uint8_t *` | MAC buffer used for CMAC output or authorize input. |
| macLen | `uint8_t` | Number of MAC bytes to read or compare. |

@subsection hcu_v1_config_state hcu_state_t Highlights
| Field | Purpose |
|-------|---------|
| `cmdInProgress` | Indicates whether a command is currently being serviced. |
| `carryType` | Tracks the active polling, interrupt, or DMA service model. |
| `dataInputPtr` / `dataOutputPtr` | Hold the current FIFO service pointers. |
| `inputCount` / `outputCount` | Track remaining bytes to push or pull. |
| `ccmConfig` / `cmacConfig` | Preserve authenticated-operation context between setup and completion. |
| `callback` / `callbackParam` | Provide asynchronous completion notification. |

@subsection hcu_v1_config_features Device Feature Macros
| Macro | Description |
|-------|-------------|
| `FEATURE_HCU_AES_CTR_ENGINE` | Enables the CTR mode public APIs. |
| `FEATURE_HCU_AES_CCM_ENGINE` | Enables CCM configuration and payload APIs. |
| `FEATURE_HCU_SM4_ENGINE` | Enables SM4 ECB APIs. |
| `FEATURE_HCU_SHA_ENGINE` | Enables SHA generation APIs. |
| `FEATURE_HCU_HAS_SHA_AUTHORIZE` | Enables SHA and HMAC authorize APIs. |
| `FEATURE_HCU_HMAC_ENGINE` | Enables HMAC key loading and wrapper APIs. |
| `FEATURE_HCU_HAS_FIXED_DMA` | Selects the DMA service path used by the driver. |
| `FEATURE_HCU_ONE_LOOP_INPUT_WATERMARK` | Input FIFO watermark used during loop service. |
| `FEATURE_HCU_ONE_LOOP_DATA_SIZE` | Number of 32-bit words moved per FIFO service iteration. |

@section hcu_v1_usage Usage Patterns

@subsection hcu_v1_usage_basic Pattern 1: Basic AES-ECB Encryption
Initialize the driver, load a user key, encrypt one block-aligned buffer, and
de-initialize the module.

@code{.c}
hcu_user_config_t hcuConfig = {
    .swap = MODE_SWAPPING_NO,
    .carryType = HCU_USING_POLLING,
    .ingressDMAChannel = 0U,
    .egressDMAChannel = 0U
};
hcu_state_t hcuState;
uint32_t aesKey[4] = { 0x00112233U, 0x44556677U, 0x8899AABBU, 0xCCDDEEFFU };
uint32_t plainText[4] = { 0x00112233U, 0x44556677U, 0x8899AABBU, 0xCCDDEEFFU };
uint32_t cipherText[4];

HCU_DRV_Init(&hcuConfig, &hcuState);
HCU_DRV_LoadUserKey(aesKey, KEY_SIZE_128_BITS);
HCU_DRV_EncryptECB(plainText, sizeof(plainText), cipherText);
HCU_DRV_DeInit(&hcuState);
@endcode

@subsection hcu_v1_usage_cbc Pattern 2: AES-CBC Processing with an IV
Use CBC mode when the application manages an initialization vector explicitly.

@code{.c}
hcu_user_config_t hcuConfig = {
    .swap = MODE_SWAPPING_NO,
    .carryType = HCU_USING_POLLING,
    .ingressDMAChannel = 0U,
    .egressDMAChannel = 0U
};
hcu_state_t hcuState;
uint32_t aesKey[4] = { 0x2B7E1516U, 0x28AED2A6U, 0xABF71588U, 0x09CF4F3CU };
uint32_t iv[4] = { 0x00010203U, 0x04050607U, 0x08090A0BU, 0x0C0D0E0FU };
uint32_t plainText[8];
uint32_t cipherText[8];

HCU_DRV_Init(&hcuConfig, &hcuState);
HCU_DRV_LoadUserKey(aesKey, KEY_SIZE_128_BITS);
HCU_DRV_EncryptCBC(plainText, sizeof(plainText), iv, cipherText);
HCU_DRV_DeInit(&hcuState);
@endcode

@subsection hcu_v1_usage_sha Pattern 3: Single-Shot SHA-256 Generation
Generate one SHA digest in polling mode using the all-in-one message type.

@code{.c}
hcu_user_config_t hcuConfig = {
    .swap = MODE_SWAPPING_NO,
    .carryType = HCU_USING_POLLING,
    .ingressDMAChannel = 0U,
    .egressDMAChannel = 0U
};
hcu_state_t hcuState;
uint32_t msg[16];
uint32_t digest[8];

HCU_DRV_Init(&hcuConfig, &hcuState);
HCU_DRV_GenerateSHA(msg, sizeof(msg), sizeof(msg), HCU_SHA_256, MSG_ALL, digest);
HCU_DRV_DeInit(&hcuState);
@endcode

@subsection hcu_v1_usage_ccm Pattern 4: AES-CCM Setup and Payload Processing
Prepare the authenticated-data context first, then process one or more payload
blocks through the CCM data path.

@code{.c}
hcu_user_config_t hcuConfig = {
    .swap = MODE_SWAPPING_NO,
    .carryType = HCU_USING_POLLING,
    .ingressDMAChannel = 0U,
    .egressDMAChannel = 0U
};
hcu_state_t hcuState;
uint8_t nonce[12] = { 0U };
uint8_t aad[16] = { 0U };
uint8_t tag[16];
uint32_t aesKey[4] = { 0U };
uint32_t plainText[4];
uint32_t cipherText[4];
hcu_ccm_config_t ccmConfig = {
    .nonce = nonce,
    .nonceSize = sizeof(nonce),
    .addData = aad,
    .addDataSize = sizeof(aad),
    .tag = tag,
    .tagSize = 16U,
    .msgLen = sizeof(plainText)
};

HCU_DRV_Init(&hcuConfig, &hcuState);
HCU_DRV_LoadUserKey(aesKey, KEY_SIZE_128_BITS);
HCU_DRV_CCMConfig(&ccmConfig, MODE_ENC);
HCU_DRV_EncryptCCM(plainText, sizeof(plainText), cipherText, true);
HCU_DRV_DeInit(&hcuState);
@endcode

@section hcu_v1_api API Quick Reference

@subsection hcu_v1_api_init Initialization & Runtime Control
| Function | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| `HCU_DRV_Init()` | Install the driver state, apply the configured swap mode, and create the completion semaphore. |
| `HCU_DRV_DeInit()` | Disable the interrupt path, clear the runtime state, and destroy the completion semaphore. |
| `HCU_DRV_CfgSwapping()` | Program the input data swap mode in the HCU control register. |
| `HCU_DRV_ClearODFlag()` | Clear the operation-done status flag through the W1C status register field. |

@subsection hcu_v1_api_key Callback & Key Management
| Function | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| `HCU_DRV_InstallCallback()` | Install or replace the completion callback used by asynchronous operation. |
| `HCU_DRV_LoadUserKey()` | Load an AES or SM4 software key into the HCU key registers. |
| `HCU_DRV_LoadHMACKey()` | Load the optional HMAC key and enable the HMAC engine. |

@subsection hcu_v1_api_cipher Cipher Operations
| Function | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| `HCU_DRV_EncryptECB()` / `HCU_DRV_DecryptECB()` | Run AES ECB processing on a 16-byte aligned message length. |
| `HCU_DRV_EncryptCBC()` / `HCU_DRV_DecryptCBC()` | Run AES CBC processing with an optional IV update. |
| `HCU_DRV_EncryptCTR()` / `HCU_DRV_DecryptCTR()` | Run AES CTR processing with an optional counter-value update. |
| `HCU_DRV_EncryptSM4ECB()` / `HCU_DRV_DecryptSM4ECB()` | Run SM4 ECB processing when the SM4 engine is available. |

@subsection hcu_v1_api_auth MAC, Hash, and Authenticated Encryption
| Function | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| `HCU_DRV_GenerateMAC()` / `HCU_DRV_AuthorizeMAC()` | Generate or verify a CMAC value over one message segment or an entire message. |
| `HCU_DRV_GenerateSHA()` / `HCU_DRV_AuthorizeSHA()` | Generate or authorize SHA-256 or SHA-384 digests. |
| `HCU_DRV_GenerateHMAC()` / `HCU_DRV_AuthorizeHMAC()` | Reuse the SHA execution path for HMAC flows when enabled. |
| `HCU_DRV_CCMConfig()` | Program nonce, AAD, tag, and total-length information for CCM. |
| `HCU_DRV_EncryptCCM()` / `HCU_DRV_DecryptCCM()` | Process CCM payload blocks after the CCM context has been configured. |

@subsection hcu_v1_api_irq Command Status & Interrupt Handling
| Function | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| `HCU_DRV_CancelCommand()` | Declared cancel hook for an active command. |
| `HCU_DRV_IRQHandler()` | Service input and output FIFO interrupts and the operation-done interrupt. |

@section hcu_v1_notes Important Notes

@subsection hcu_v1_notes_align Buffer Alignment and Length Rules
- AES, SM4, CMAC, and CCM payload buffers are required to be 32-bit aligned.
- AES, SM4, and CCM payload lengths must be multiples of 16 bytes.
- `HCU_DRV_LoadUserKey()` and `HCU_DRV_LoadHMACKey()` also expect 32-bit aligned key buffers.

@subsection hcu_v1_notes_sha SHA Segmentation Rules
- When `msgType` is `MSG_START` or `MSG_MIDDLE`, SHA-256 message chunks must be non-zero multiples of 64 bytes.
- When `msgType` is `MSG_START` or `MSG_MIDDLE`, SHA-384 message chunks must be non-zero multiples of 128 bytes.
- `totalLen` is programmed only on the first or all-in-one SHA block.

@subsection hcu_v1_notes_ccm CCM Preparation Rules
- `HCU_DRV_CCMConfig()` must complete successfully before calling `HCU_DRV_EncryptCCM()` or `HCU_DRV_DecryptCCM()`.
- CCM tag length must be even and must not exceed 16 bytes.
- The CCM nonce length must be compatible with the encoded `L` field; otherwise the driver returns `STATUS_HCU_CCM_NONCE_DATA_SIZE_ERROR`.

@subsection hcu_v1_notes_async Carry Mode Behavior
- Polling mode blocks inside the public API until FIFO service and completion handling finish.
- Interrupt mode enables the required FIFO watermark and operation-done interrupts, then returns after starting the engine.
- DMA mode configures ingress and egress channels, starts the command, and completes through DMA callbacks and the HCU interrupt path.

@subsection hcu_v1_notes_busy Busy-State and Completion Behavior
- Public processing APIs return `STATUS_BUSY` when the HCU hardware or driver state is already occupied.
- Loading a user or HMAC key while a command is active returns `STATUS_HCU_LOAD_KEY_WHEN_BUSY`.
- For CMAC, CCM, SHA authorize, and related asynchronous flows, completion status may be delivered through the registered callback.

@section hcu_v1_integration Integration Guide

@subsection hcu_v1_integration_units Compilation Units
- `platform/drivers/inc/hcu_v1/hcu_driver.h`
- `platform/drivers/src/hcu_v1/hcu_driver.c`
- `platform/drivers/src/hcu_v1/hcu_hw_access.h`
- `platform/drivers/src/hcu_v1/hcu_irq.c`
- `platform/drivers/src/hcu_v1/doxygen/hcu.dox`

@subsection hcu_v1_integration_includes Include Paths
- Include the public API with `#include "hcu_driver.h"`.
- The driver source depends on `interrupt_manager.h`, `core_common.h`, and `hcu_hw_access.h`.

@subsection hcu_v1_integration_deps Dependencies
- OS abstraction: `osif.h`
- DMA services: `dma_driver.h`
- Callback type definitions: `callbacks.h`
- Device register definitions and feature macros: `device_registers.h`
- Interrupt services: `interrupt_manager.h`

@subsection hcu_v1_integration_build Build Considerations
- Enable the HCU peripheral clock and any device-level security configuration required by the target.
- Keep the feature guards aligned with the device header so unavailable algorithms are not referenced by the application.
- Route the startup interrupt vector to `HCU_IRQHandler()` when interrupt or DMA carry modes are used.
*/
